· Streamlit → 8501

Step 3: Run the container

# Pattern:  -p HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT

docker run -d --name myapp -p 8000:8000 app:v1.0

docker run -d --name myapp -p 8000:8000 --env-file .env app:v1.0


Step 4: Verify it's running

docker ps
docker logs myapp


Step 5: Open in browser

· Flask → http://localhost:5000
· FastAPI → http://localhost:8000/docs
· Node → http://localhost:3000
· Spring → http://localhost:8080


# Useful lifecycle commands


docker stop myapp            # stop
docker start myapp           # restart
docker restart myapp         # bounce
docker rm myapp              # remove (must stop first)
docker rm -f myapp           # force remove




Part 2 — Share Your Image

Pick one of two methods:

Method A — Offline (tar file) — easiest for classroom

# Save image to a tar file
docker save app:v1.0 -o app-v1.0.tar

# Compress (recommended if >200 MB)
gzip app-v1.0.tar
# → produces app-v1.0.tar.gz


Share the file via:

· USB drive
· Google Drive / OneDrive / WeTransfer
· Slack / WhatsApp / Teams
· Copy to a shared network folder

The recipient loads it:

# If .tar
docker load -i app-v1.0.tar

# If .tar.gz
docker load -i app-v1.0.tar.gz
# or
gunzip -c app-v1.0.tar.gz | docker load


Verify:

docker images | grep app


Method B — Docker Hub (online, proper way)

# 1. Create account at https://hub.docker.com (free)

# 2. Login
docker login
# username: <their-dockerhub-username>
# password: <their-password or access token>

# 3. Tag the image with their namespace
#    Replace 'studentname' with their Docker Hub username
docker tag app:v1.0 studentname/app:v1.0
docker tag app:v1.0 studentname/app:latest

# 4. Push
docker push studentname/app:v1.0
docker push studentname/app:latest

# 5. Verify on https://hub.docker.com/u/studentname


Anyone can now pull:


docker pull studentname/app:v1.0
docker run -d --name their-app -p 8001:8000 studentname/app:v1.0


